think surveyed Comlink first — the sealed datagram wire plus a discovery subsystem (descriptor = key·transport·freshness·lineage, a bounded peer table); nothing read a rotation yet, and the turn tilak I just built is exactly that freshness+key
think built comlink/turn_route.rye — the seam where identity's rotation meets the network's routing; it imports the Turn shape and verify_turn from kumara/tilak.rye via a symlink (comlink/kumara_tilak.rye), whose own nested import of the seam resolves to comlink/kumara.rye
think two rules, both from the tilak's own law — authentic before routed (a turn is trusted only when the peer's identity key signed it; a forgery keeps the last trusted key) and fresher wins with freshness only climbing (a reset outranks any key rotation by its higher reset_counter; within an era the higher key_counter wins; a non-newer turn never supersedes, so no replay rolls a peer's key backward)
think read_turn returns a RouteDecision — accepted, the route_key to seal the wire with, and a reason (verified_first · verified_fresher · stale_kept · forged_refused)
think added a small pubkey_of helper to the tilak so a caller gets an identity's public key without the whole keypair; kumara regressions stayed GREEN
obs the selftest proves the whole decision — first turn taken, a fresher turn rotates the key, a reset outranks even with key_counter back at zero, a replayed old turn is kept out, a forged turn (signed by another key) is refused and the key unchanged
obs tame_style_check clean on turn_route.rye; the Rishi witness drives the built binary and checks each branch
